    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Atmospheric chemistry",
    "problem": "Atmospheric chemistry models estimate small historical changes in the atmosphere's oxidative capacity, but proxy-based observations suggest much larger changes, indicating that models underestimate the sensitivity of atmospheric oxidants to climate and emissions.",
    "method": "The authors propose using multiple paleo-chemistry proxies from ice cores, particularly the oxygen (\u0394\u00b9\u2077O) and nitrogen (\u03b4\u00b9\u2075N) isotopes in nitrate and CO, to provide robust constraints on past atmospheric oxidative capacity and thereby inform models about potential future changes.",
    "task": "The task is to review and synthesize paleo-observational and modeling evidence to understand and constrain uncertainties in predicting future changes to the atmosphere's oxidative capacity due to climate change and anthropogenic emissions.",
